item.text:
Chapter 4B research wave completed; no score assigned and no other chapter researched.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico - November 2022","publisher":"International IDEA","publication_date":"2022-11","url":"https://www.idea.int/democracytracker/report/mexico/november-2022","claim":"López Obrador’s proposed electoral reform would alter appointments, reduce the electoral authority’s budget, and eliminate local electoral bodies; civil society organized mass demonstrations against it.","locator":"Mexico November 2022 entry, reform paragraphs","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-001","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.idea.int/democracytracker/report/mexico/november-2022|electoral reform|appointments budget local authorities","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2","4B.4","4B.10"],"source_type":"intergovernmental_monitor","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"International IDEA directly documented the proposal and public response during the target period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly concerns November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The reform was announced and promoted by López Obrador and his administration; institutional effects remained proposed or legislative at this point.","contrary_evidence":["The constitutional reform faced a two-thirds-majority requirement and organized civil-society opposition.","The INE and electoral courts remained operational during 2022."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.4","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"López Obrador overhauls Mexican electoral system, sparking protests","publisher":"The Washington Post","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2022/12/15/lopez-obrador-electoral-reform-mexico/","claim":"Congress passed legislation reducing the powers and staffing of the National Electoral Institute, prompting large protests; López Obrador was constitutionally barred from seeking a second term.","locator":"Report paragraphs 1-7","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-002","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2022/12/15/lopez-obrador-electoral-reform-mexico/|INE cuts and protests|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"],"source_type":"reputable_press","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting described the congressional vote, protests, institutional concerns, and constitutional term limit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The legislation was the president’s initiative and was signed or intended for signature by his administration, while Congress enacted it.","contrary_evidence":["The president could not constitutionally seek immediate reelection.","Opposition parties, civil society, and courts retained avenues to contest the changes."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.8","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Freedom in the World 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-world/2022","claim":"Mexico remained Partly Free, with López Obrador repeatedly attacking critics, media, civil-society organizations, and autonomous institutions; press diversity persisted, but threats, self-censorship, impunity, and dependence on government advertising constrained media freedom.","locator":"Country overview lines 59-87; media sections lines 165-168","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-003","canonical_fact_key":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-world/2022|Partly Free and attacks on critics|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.10"],"source_type":"comparative_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established comparative-monitoring organization with explicit political-rights and civil-liberties assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The report evaluates the 2021-2022 period and is used here for 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"The report directly attributes repeated presidential rhetoric to López Obrador, while media violence and impunity involve state and non-state actors.","contrary_evidence":["Independent media and diverse viewpoints remained active.","The federal protection mechanism continued providing protection to journalists and defenders, though inadequately."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Mexico","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html","claim":"Independent Mexican media remained active and expressed diverse views, but presidential denunciations of alleged fake news, attacks and killings of journalists, impunity, and self-censorship limited effective press freedom.","locator":"Freedom of Expression section, report lines 369-412","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-004","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089118.html|independent media and journalist violence|Mexico 2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.3","4B.6"],"source_type":"government_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed country report drawing on government, civil-society, and public reporting; it distinguishes legal protection from practical restrictions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Report covers 2022 conditions.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes the morning-conference attacks on alleged fake-news journalists directly to the president; killings and attacks have mixed state, criminal, and local causes.","contrary_evidence":["Independent media operated without blanket formal censorship.","The government maintained a protection mechanism and announced measures against impunity."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico.CIVICUSMonitor.June2022","publisher":"CIVICUS Monitor","publication_date":"2022-06","url":"https://civicus.contentfiles.net/media/assets/file/Mexico.CIVICUSMonitor.June2022.pdf","claim":"CIVICUS rated Mexican civic space as Repressed, reporting 11 journalists killed in the first five months of 2022, presidential stigmatization of critics and feminists, and a proposed Morena tax measure that could restrict foreign funding for civil-society organizations.","locator":"Pages 1-2, civic-space, violence, funding-restrictions, and stigmatization sections","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-005","canonical_fact_key":"https://civicus.contentfiles.net/media/assets/file/Mexico.CIVICUSMonitor.June2022.pdf|repressed civic space and CSO restrictions|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.10"],"source_type":"civic_space_monitor","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized civic-space monitor with dated 2022 observations and source-based indicators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"First five months and legislative context of 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The report directly attributes stigmatizing rhetoric to López Obrador and identifies the Morena-sponsored funding proposal; journalist violence has mixed perpetrators.","contrary_evidence":["The funding measure was a proposal, not established law in the cited period.","Civil society remained organized and capable of public advocacy and protest."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report of the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights 2022: Mexico","publisher":"Organization of American States / IACHR Special Rapporteurship for Freedom of Expression","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf","claim":"The IACHR documented continuing attacks and threats against journalists, widespread fear and self-censorship, and repeated stigmatizing statements by public officials, including the executive’s “Who’s Who in Lies of the Week” segment.","locator":"PDF pages 92-93, paragraphs 391-392","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-006","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oas.org/en/iachr/docs/annual/2022/Chapters/14-IA2022_Cap_5_MX_EN.pdf|press hostility and stigmatization|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.3","4B.6"],"source_type":"regional_human_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Regional human-rights rapporteur report with cited incidents and institutional findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Annual 2022 country chapter.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes stigmatizing executive communications to government spokespersons and the executive branch; criminal attacks remain multi-actor.","contrary_evidence":["The IACHR recognized that government authorities may legitimately respond publicly to alleged misinformation, but found the repeated stigmatizing pattern harmful.","The state reported protection and investigative measures."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Army used Pegasus spyware against journalists and activists","publisher":"ARTICLE 19, R3D and SocialTIC","publication_date":"2022-10-03","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-army-spyware-journalists-activists/","claim":"Digital-rights organizations, with Citizen Lab forensic support, reported Pegasus infections of journalists and a human-rights defender during the López Obrador administration, contradicting presidential assurances that the government no longer had relations with Pegasus.","locator":"Release summary and concluding demands","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-007","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-army-spyware-journalists-activists/|Pegasus infections during current administration|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.9","4B.6"],"source_type":"digital_rights_investigation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Forensic findings were supported by Citizen Lab and jointly published by established digital-rights organizations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published October 2022; reported infections occurred during the current administration, including 2021.","ruler_attribution":"Evidence links the surveillance cases to military or state infrastructure but does not establish that López Obrador personally ordered each operation.","contrary_evidence":["The president denied that journalists or opponents were being spied on and described the activity as intelligence work.","The organizations called for investigation precisely because authorization and accountability remained unresolved."],"lenses":["4B.9","4B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pegasus: López Obrador: “No es cierto que se espíe a periodistas u opositores”","publisher":"El País México","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://elpais.com/mexico/2022-10-04/lopez-obrador-no-es-cierto-que-se-espie-a-periodistas-u-opositores-son-labores-de-inteligencia-no-de-espionaje.html","claim":"After reporting that Pegasus had targeted journalist Ricardo Raphael, an Animal Político journalist, and defender Raymundo Ramos during his administration, López Obrador denied espionage and characterized the activity as intelligence work.","locator":"Article paragraph describing the three targets and presidential response","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-008","canonical_fact_key":"https://elpais.com/mexico/2022-10-04/lopez-obrador-no-es-cierto-que-se-espie-a-periodistas-u-opositores-son-labores-de-inteligencia-no-de-espionaje.html|presidential denial and intelligence framing|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.9"],"source_type":"local_language_press","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Mexican reporting quoting the president and identifying the organizations’ allegations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential statement; it establishes stated intent and denial, not whether the denial was factually correct.","contrary_evidence":["ARTICLE 19, R3D, SocialTIC, and Citizen Lab reported forensic evidence of infections.","The identity of the authorizing official remained unresolved in the source record."],"lenses":["4B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe de resultados sobre el análisis de la operación y participación en casillas especiales en la Jornada de Revocación de Mandato 2022","publisher":"Instituto Nacional Electoral","publication_date":"2022-08-24","url":"https://repositoriodocumental.ine.mx/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/141875/cotspel-2so-24-08-2022-p04.4.pdf","claim":"The INE’s post-process report documented the organization of special polling stations, party-representative attendance, and operational participation data for the April 2022 recall process.","locator":"PDF pages 17-22, especially tables 6 and 8","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-009","canonical_fact_key":"https://repositoriodocumental.ine.mx/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/141875/cotspel-2so-24-08-2022-p04.4.pdf|special polling stations and party representatives|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.1","4B.4"],"source_type":"official_electoral_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary election-management report with operational tables and documented procedures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Recall process held 10 April 2022; report dated August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the electoral institution’s independent administration and party access; it does not establish presidential intent.","contrary_evidence":["Party-representative attendance at special polling stations was limited.","The recall was non-binding because turnout remained below the constitutional threshold."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Informe final sobre la implementación de la metodología de promoción y difusión de la participación ciudadana de la Revocación de Mandato 2022","publisher":"Instituto Nacional Electoral","publication_date":"2022-05-26","url":"https://repositoriodocumental.ine.mx/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/135992/cotspel-8se-26-05-2022-p18.pdf","claim":"The INE reported balanced discussion forums, public information campaigns, and extensive outreach for the recall process, including millions of digital impressions and materials for voters abroad and indigenous-language audiences.","locator":"PDF pages 15-16 and 31-32; metrics tables","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-010","canonical_fact_key":"https://repositoriodocumental.ine.mx/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/135992/cotspel-8se-26-05-2022-p18.pdf|recall information and balanced forums|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.1","4B.4","4B.7"],"source_type":"official_electoral_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary INE implementation report with statutory references and dissemination metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Recall preparation and implementation in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Evidence concerns the electoral authority’s process, not personal presidential conduct.","contrary_evidence":["The process had very low turnout and limited party-representative attendance in some special polling stations.","The report does not independently establish equal campaigning by the president’s supporters and opponents."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4","4B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sala Superior 306/2022","publisher":"Tribunal Electoral del Poder Judicial de la Federación","publication_date":"2022-09-14","url":"https://www.te.gob.mx/front3/bulletins/detail/4710/0","claim":"The Electoral Tribunal held that alleged irregularities and violence in a contested 2022 election were not sufficiently extensive or determinative to invalidate the result, applying constitutional electoral standards through judicial review.","locator":"Bulletin 306/2022, summary of the Superior Chamber decision","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-011","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.te.gob.mx/front3/bulletins/detail/4710/0|electoral irregularities not determinative|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.1","4B.4"],"source_type":"official_electoral_judgment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary electoral-court decision summary concerning a 2022 election challenge.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 decision concerning a 2022 election.","ruler_attribution":"Demonstrates operation of an independent electoral adjudicator; it does not directly establish presidential control or non-control.","contrary_evidence":["The tribunal acknowledged irregularities and violence, even while finding them insufficiently determinative.","The case was a subnational election rather than a presidential contest."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexiko 2022 | Amnesty International Report 2022/23","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2023-03-28","url":"https://www.amnesty.de/informieren/amnesty-report/mexiko-2022","claim":"Amnesty reported continued stigmatization of feminists and human-rights defenders, violent security-force responses to women’s protests in some states, record-level journalist killings, and extensive expansion of National Guard responsibilities into traditionally civilian areas.","locator":"Mexico 2022 summary paragraphs","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-012","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.amnesty.de/informieren/amnesty-report/mexiko-2022|stigmatization protests journalists civilian functions|2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.7","4B.10"],"source_type":"international_rights_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established human-rights organization with country-specific annual reporting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Annual assessment of 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The report identifies government rhetoric and security-force conduct, but responsibility is distributed across federal and state authorities.","contrary_evidence":["Some protections and public mechanisms for journalists and defenders remained in place.","The report does not show that every protest or minority-rights outcome was centrally directed by the president."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.7","4B.10"]}

Reused evidence from the supplied index: 8 records, principally the recall results, electricity-reform defeat, National Guard legislation and judicial review, Ayotzinapa process, RSF press-freedom assessment, HRW, and Amnesty’s militarization findings. New accepted units: 12.

Research accounting:

- Documents discovered: approximately 35 plausible documents.
- Documents opened: 22 underlying pages, reports, legal records, or PDFs.
- Accepted: 12 new source-claim units.
- Rejected: 8 for duplication, weak attribution, out-of-period focus, or insufficiently recoverable detail.
- Access-blocked: 5 pages/PDFs, including one ARTICLE 19 page, one UNAM legal PDF, one SCJN page, and two cached reports.
- Distinct organizations: at least 12.
- Source types: official electoral records and judgments, intergovernmental monitoring, government human-rights reporting, NGO/civic-space reports, digital-rights investigations, academic work, and local/international press.

Lens coverage:

- 4B.1: covered through INE recall administration/results and TEPJF review; no competitive national election occurred in 2022.
- 4B.2: covered through proposed and enacted electoral changes, INE concerns, and Pegasus allegations.
- 4B.3: covered through opposition protests, independent media activity, civic-space reporting, and presidential criticism.
- 4B.4: covered through INE procedures, TEPJF adjudication, congressional resistance, and electoral-institution threats.
- 4B.5: covered through presidential attacks on critics, civil society, feminists, and autonomous institutions.
- 4B.6: strongly covered through IACHR, USDOS, Freedom House, RSF-reused evidence, and ARTICLE 19.
- 4B.7: partially covered through feminist and civic-space evidence; direct 2022 evidence on minorities and opposition regions remains limited.
- 4B.8: covered by the constitutional bar on immediate reelection and the non-binding recall process; actual succession under AMLO was not tested in 2022.
- 4B.9: covered by Pegasus investigations and the president’s denial; authorization responsibility remains unresolved.
- 4B.10: covered through inherited press-security and impunity problems plus evidence of institutional stress during 2022; attribution of the net trajectory remains mixed.

I could not update `research-ledger-manifest.json`: the workspace filesystem was exposed as read-only and shell access failed before the manifest could be inspected. The 12 new manifest entries are reproduced above so they can be merged without replacing prior entries.

Another chapter-specific search wave would have limited material value. The main residual gaps are direct evidence on minority political equality, nationwide opposition treatment, and formal presidential responsibility for Pegasus operations. Further searching could refine those gaps, but is unlikely to materially change the already convergent evidence environment without access to additional primary records.
